The **Euler line** of a triangle passes through the circumcenter $ O $, centroid $ G $, and orthocenter $ H $. The **Euler line** is also known to pass through the incenter $ I $.

But here we are given that $ IA \perp OI $, which is a very specific condition.

This is a **special case** that relates the incenter $ I $ to the **perpendicular from $ A $ to $ OI $**.

